# An upper bound of a b, for radii: the rounded product, one step up. For nonnegative operands it
# exceeds the exact product by at most 3/2 gaps, which is sharp (a product halfway between two
# numbers of the format that rounds up). For operands of any sign exhaustive runs give 2; not
# claimed.
kernel:        rmul
input:         a
input:         b
output:        r
value:         a*b
requires:      nonneg a
requires:      nonneg b
ensures:       upper r gaps=3/2
min_precision: 2
theorem:       proofs/theories/Directed.v rmul_correct
source:        one step up from the rounded product (Milestone 1 listing)
license:       algorithm and proof: MIT
status:        proved
cone_exempt:   r = succ(p), and succ(-inf) is finite; r is a radius, and the ball layer tests radii for non-finite values (Milestone 1 statements, Ball kernels)

p = prod(a, b)
r = succ(p)
